WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Least privilege, three ways.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:07.896
Least privilege means each part of the system gets the narrowest access that still does its job.

3
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:10.523
For an agent, apply it on three axes.

4
00:00:10.803 --> 00:00:11.656
Tools.

5
00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:14.624
Give the agent only the tools the task needs.

6
00:00:14.904 --> 00:00:24.653
OWASP’s Excessive Agency entry lists excessive functionality as a root cause: an extension that only needs to read documents but can also delete them.

7
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:34.192
Anthropic’s Managed Agents security guide puts it as a default: start the agent toolset with every tool disabled, then enable the ones you need.

8
00:00:34.472 --> 00:00:39.741
A tool that is not loaded cannot be misused, and it also costs no context tokens.

9
00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:41.024
Credentials.

10
00:00:41.304 --> 00:00:44.632
The tool’s identity should be scoped to the task, not to you.

11
00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:52.955
A status-report agent needs read access to issues and permission to post in one channel, not an admin token for the whole tracker.

12
00:00:53.235 --> 00:01:02.045
GitHub’s fine-grained personal access tokens, which you’ll use in the project, can be limited to specific repositories and specific permissions.

13
00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:13.376
The Managed Agents guide goes further: keep credentials in a vault that injects them into outbound requests, so the secret never sits in the sandbox where the agent could read and leak it.

14
00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:14.467
Network.

15
00:01:14.747 --> 00:01:21.829
Most damage from a fooled agent leaves through the network: an email, an HTTP request, a rendered image.

16
00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:24.669
Restrict egress to the hosts the task needs.

17
00:01:24.949 --> 00:01:40.224
The Managed Agents guide’s recommended environment setting is limited networking with only the hosts the agent needs, and Claude Code’s sandbox routes every connection through a proxy that checks the host against your allowed domains, which start empty.

18
00:01:40.504 --> 00:01:45.752
Least privilege has one more benefit that matters to a PM: it makes the risk legible.

19
00:01:46.032 --> 00:01:53.179
“This agent can read issues in two repos and post to one channel” is a sentence a security reviewer can approve.

20
00:01:53.459 --> 00:01:55.549
“This agent has my token” is not.
